WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.110
It is my pleasure to welcome to the program
today, Dr. Peniel Joseph, who's the founding

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.390
director of the Center for the Study of Race
and Democracy.

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.160
Professor of history at the University of
Texas at Austin.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.299
Also author of the book The Sword and the
Shield The Revolutionary Lives of Malcolm

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:18.570
X and Martin Luther King Junior.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.000
So great to have you on.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.820
Thank you for having me.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:31.039
So to begin with, we've seen over the last
four to six months some of the larger protests

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:32.460
that this country has seen.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:38.940
I guess we can say in the sort of medium term
since the Occupy Wall Street days of some

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.540
years ago.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:48.190
And one of the great debates that has surfaced
is in the context of the teachings of Malcolm

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.719
X and the teachings of Martin Luther King.

00:00:51.719 --> 00:00:56.430
What would they say about the protests today?

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:02.640
What would their views be about the tactics
that would be considered either valid or moral

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.299
or productive for achieving change today?

00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:13.479
And and my instinct is that a lot of people
are ascribing to these two men what they already

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:15.479
believe to be the right answer.

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:19.289
And so I want to start by going back to the
history.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:21.950
Where can how can we frame this conversation?

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:28.779
Well, I think the best way to frame it is
to look at the convergences between Malcolm

00:01:28.779 --> 00:01:31.209
X and Martin Luther King Junior.

00:01:31.209 --> 00:01:37.189
So even though we think of Malcolm as the
political sword of the 1960s and we think

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:42.479
of King as sort of this nonviolent human rights
shield, one of the things I argue in my new

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:44.989
book is that they really converge.

00:01:44.989 --> 00:01:47.689
Malcolm X is arguing for radical black dignity.

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:50.069
King is arguing for radical black citizenship.

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:54.669
And over time, they come to use that political
sword and shield.

00:01:54.669 --> 00:01:58.829
Malcolm X goes from talking about self-defense.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:03.070
And certainly this global revolution to saying
that the civil rights movement should be a

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.789
human rights movement.

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:09.429
And he talks about the ballot or the bullet
and actually used it using voting rights and

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.519
using democratic institutions.

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:15.260
But he also always reserves the right of indigenous
people for self-defense.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:22.780
A king goes from talking about nonviolence,
and he's always talking about no use of violence.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.099
But he starts to look at civil disobedience
as a tool.

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:29.760
He calls it a nonviolent political sword.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.209
By 1965.

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.930
That could paralyze cities in order to achieve
racial justice and human rights for black

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.790
people, but for all people.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.790
Right.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:44.880
So when we think about the Black Lives Matter
movement with that movement, does is it uses

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:51.170
both King's notion of radical black citizenship
and Malcolm X, his notion of radical black

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.170
dignity.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.980
And we think about what it Malcolm mean by
that.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.920
He meant eradicating systemic racism and defeating
white supremacy globally and allowing black

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.349
people to have political self-determination.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:07.319
King is going to say that citizenship is more
than voting rights.

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:13.870
It's actually a right to an income, a guaranteed
job, affordable housing.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.609
King talks about food, justice and, of course,
dies.

00:03:16.609 --> 00:03:22.719
Trying to lead a poor people's campaign took
Occupy Washington, D.C.. David.

00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:28.730
So you mentioned Occupy Wall Street, but King
tried to occupy Washington, D.C. in 1968.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:35.030
One of the things in the last six months looking
at interviews from decades ago, but with both

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:39.709
Malcolm X and Martin Luther King, one of the
refrains that I frequently heard from Martin

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:45.030
Luther King that I didn't find Malcolm X using,
and I'm curious whether it is it is actually

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.670
a distinction or maybe I didn't look hard
enough.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:54.560
Was Martin Luther King asserting that one
of the risks sometimes of going over into

00:03:54.560 --> 00:04:02.200
violence is that it ends up disproportionately
hurting the very people who these Moate movements

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.560
are actually trying to help?

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.889
So sort of a two part question.

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.420
Is it true that that is a difference and that
we did not hear that specific idea from Malcolm

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:12.420
X?

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.469
And is it is it accurate?

00:04:14.469 --> 00:04:19.139
Why say that Malcolm talks about even Steven?

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:21.370
He talks about an eye for an eye?

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:26.830
I think when we think about Malcolm X, he
talks about the man now what happened in Kenya.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:33.520
He's really thinking about and supporting
anticolonial movements, anti imperialist movements.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.699
He supports the Cuban revolution.

00:04:35.699 --> 00:04:37.420
He supports the Algerian revolution.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:41.210
He's supporting revolutions all over the place.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:47.620
So he's not saying exactly what King says
there, where, look, if we were to achieve

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:53.970
or try to enact guerrilla warfare, we're going
to suffer the disproportionate losses, which

00:04:53.970 --> 00:05:00.560
I think King is correct in in saying I think
Malcolm's more thinking about this global

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.520
political revolution.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:09.020
And remember, Malcolm's alive during the age
where Africa is decolonizing, where Latin

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.400
America and the Caribbean and South America.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.500
This is you think about from the 1940s all
the way into the 80s, in the 90s.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:19.000
The world is really on fire.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Right?

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.660
The last African country is gonna be South
Africa with the freedom of Nelson Mandela.

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:27.400
But we think about Central America, Latin
America, the Caribbean.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.270
There are still battles being waged when we
think about Nicaragua.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.449
We think about El Salvador.

00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:41.220
When we think about the world, the world continues
to Brazil and different parts of South America.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.669
There continues to be unrest and upheaval
where indigenous people are arguing for the

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:49.840
right for what political self-determination
and liberation and freedom.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.660
So Malcolm's always on that side.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.800
I do think what we'll see where they really
converge.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:00.289
By 1964 is that Malcolm who takes his HOJ
to Mecca.

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:03.819
And remember, Malcolm is a part of this global
Islamic community.

00:06:03.819 --> 00:06:04.819
Right.

00:06:04.819 --> 00:06:05.840
Malcolm was always a Muslim.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:10.910
Whether he's in the nation of Islam or he
takes the hij, but he starts to say that sincere

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.990
whites can be part of the movement.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:13.990
Right.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:19.300
If whites are willing to give up white privilege
and racism, they can be part of this movement,

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.409
which he calls a global human rights movement.

00:06:21.409 --> 00:06:27.180
And he also says that this at Oxford University,
David, in 1964, one of the best debates and

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:29.389
the BBC carries this.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:32.190
This debate where Malcolm receives a standing
ovation.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.980
Does a 30 minute debate.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.390
And he's actually it's this is very this is
an interesting historical nugget.

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:42.850
He's actually defending Barry Goldwater's
phrase.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.789
Extremism in defense of liberty is no vice.

00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:48.630
But for different ideological reasons than
Barry Goldwater.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:49.630
Right.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.290
Barry Goldwater is the conservative hero of
the American right.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:57.639
And he used that term to defend racial segregation.

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:04.360
Malcolm used the term extremism in defense
of liberty is no vice to defend his atom.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:11.669
His adage of by any means necessary liberation,
by any means necessary freedom, citizenship,

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:13.189
by any means necessary.

00:07:13.189 --> 00:07:17.960
And he tells the students, multiracial audience
at Oxford University.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.039
He says that he's willing to align with any
person, no matter what the color of their

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:24.039
skin.

00:07:24.039 --> 00:07:28.600
As long as they want to end the miserable
condition on the face of this earth.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:32.780
Now, I always found those words very, very
profound for Malcolm, because you can see

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:34.090
just the evolution.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:36.620
You see the evolution with King.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.229
King becomes this man on fire.

00:07:38.229 --> 00:07:42.670
The last three years of his life, I mean,
he's an anti imperialist.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:43.670
He's a radical.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:47.710
He becomes a revolution revolutionary where
he breaks with the Johnson administration.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.940
The Riverside Church speech, April 4th, 1967,
in New York.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:56.139
King says that the United States of America
is the greatest purveyor of violence in the

00:07:56.139 --> 00:07:57.139
world.

00:07:57.139 --> 00:08:00.939
And he's he says there comes a time when silence
is betrayal.

00:08:00.939 --> 00:08:01.939
David.

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:05.310
And he says that it's going to be a bitter
but beautiful struggle.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.870
And he criticizes the United States.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:11.380
He says, people told me to be nonviolent in
Selma.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:15.460
And when I say be nonviolent in Hanoi and
they're bombing and they're they're putting

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:18.050
napalm on children, on children.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:20.550
They say that I'm anti-American.

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:26.070
So he tells us that the three biggest evils
facing humanity are militarism, racism and

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:27.860
materialism.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.530
So King is this extraordinary figure.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:35.940
And King by 67 is always also saying that
rioting is the language of the unheard of

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.080
the oppressed.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.840
So everybody who's saying that right now,
Dr. King, would be a..

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.080
BLM or anti these protests.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:46.660
They have it exactly wrong.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:53.320
Dr. King said the roots of unrest and chaos
in the United States were based on white supremacy

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:54.470
and poverty.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:58.790
He says it's the Kerner Commission in October
of 1967.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:05.260
He says it during a Southern Christian Leadership
Conference keynote speech where he says the

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:09.190
halls of the U.S. Congress are running wild
with racism.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:13.560
He says that before a speech to the American
Psychological Association, where he says the

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:20.390
biggest threat to American democracy is white
violence and white chaos, and white people

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:25.530
are hypocritically saying that if there was
peace, there could be racial progress.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:33.280
But they are the origins of the chaos in Watts
in Detroit everywhere, terms of police brutality,

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.220
poverty, racial segregation.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:36.320
So we think about King.

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:41.600
King is not this milquetoast nice guy is giving
people hugs.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:44.060
He's a nonviolent revolutionary.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:45.430
And we stop king.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:46.490
Our memory.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:47.550
National memory.

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:52.310
It stops with Selma or sometimes stops in
63 with the march on Washington because we

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:55.120
don't want to face the revolutionary kings.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:00.490
So clearly, the some of the stories that we
we are told about both Malcolm X and Martin

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.340
Luther King are made are certainly oversimplified.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:06.440
And maybe one note when the reality is is
more complicated.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.710
I want to go back to something you said.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:15.920
You talked about the the the sort of guidelines
that Malcolm X expressed about what in what

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.691
way can white folks credibly be be part of
the movement?

00:10:18.691 --> 00:10:24.400
And I'm really interested in that, because
the general question I want to ask you is,

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:30.060
what do you believe the views of these two
men would be about modern identity politics?

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:36.020
And the view I've expressed before is that
on the one hand, using identity as either

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:40.170
a cudgel or a way to silence people.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.250
I think is is is a disaster.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:47.380
An example that I've pointed out was the way
in which the women's march leadership kept

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:51.730
out Jewish women because they were not intersectional
enough in the right way.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:53.470
I'm very much against that.

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:58.050
On the other hand, our identity informs our
experience.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:05.200
And of course, we should be paying particular
attention to what one's experience has to

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:07.490
say about solving a particular problem.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:11.880
And I think that that's where the right goes
wrong in this kind of umbrella denunciation

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.320
of identity as even something relevant to
talk about.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.540
So all of that being said, what how would
these two men, Malcolm X and Martin Luther

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:24.220
King, view what in today's language is called
identity politics?

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:28.890
Yeah, I think that they would be supportive,
but they would want new wants to it.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:29.890
Right.

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:32.700
So Malcolm Martin, people like James Baldwin.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.150
And here's where, you know, I probably would
say when we think about identity politics,

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:40.730
David, historically, there's always been identity
politics in the United States.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:47.370
It's just that they were based on SIST gender,
white male wasps who were very, very rich,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:52.360
which, by the way, still exists in some movements
on the American right, like the Tea Party

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.460
movement and others.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.460
Absolutely.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:55.460
Yeah.

00:11:55.460 --> 00:11:59.300
So these these these things in a de facto
way and sometimes in a very vicious way.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:03.560
We're anti Semitic, anti black, anti woman
anti.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.150
People of color, anti-war.

00:12:05.150 --> 00:12:08.730
Folks who are nonces gender, who are LGBTQ.

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.930
So we have to say that.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:15.460
And what we did with that original identity
politics, David, is that we said that was

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:16.460
universal.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.180
That was the universality.

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:24.420
That identity, whether it's Washington or
Jefferson, was the key to the world to unlock

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:25.750
human potential.

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:28.110
And that's why when we would read.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.570
Faulkner or Walt Whitman?

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:31.570
You think Shakespeare?

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:34.510
Not necessarily even reading stuff by white
women.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.350
When you thought with the classics were right.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.050
You're not reading stuff by Jews.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:40.130
You're not reading stuff by Muslims.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:41.730
You're not reading stuff by black people.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:46.610
So we have to say the original, but the original
identity politics has everything to do with

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:47.610
race.

00:12:47.610 --> 00:13:00.440
But it's it's a white supremacist, very elitist,
classist, hetero normative identity politics.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:11.170
We're going to pause our conversation with
Dr. Peniel Joseph.

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:35.010
They're the full interview and it

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:39.220
is extensive.

00:13:39.220 --> 00:14:08.620
We'll be available on

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:35.740
my YouTube channel, YouTube dot

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:44.870
com slash The David Pakman Show

